buying airline tickets can be maddening  but new studies are providing clues into  the inner workings of airline ticket  pricing and some of the findings may  surprise you here are four things you  need to know before buying your next  airline ticket  number one non stops are cheaper in the  past fights with one or more stops have  had lower fares than the most convenient  non-stop options but those days are over  for the most part the reason is simple  airline flights are full airlines would  rather fill seats on each flight with  non-stop passengers since they don't  have to split the fare between two or  more flights take a trip between Boston  and San Francisco for example if the  airline sells one passenger a foreigner  dollar ticket between Boston and say  Dallas and another passenger a foreigner  dollar ticket between Dallas and San  Francisco it ends up with twice as much  money than one passenger on a $400  connecting flight from Boston to San  Francisco it doesn't always work out  that way but for the most part you can  now click that non-stop only box with  pride and confidence and still save  money number two stay over on Saturdays  a study by Expedia and Airlines  reporting Corp compared trips in the  same markets that included a Saturday  night and trips that didn't they found  that while staying over on a Saturday  doesn't guarantee Yolo fare on average  airline tickets were 25% less expensive  if you included a Saturday night's stay  number three forget about oil prices  fuel prices account for roughly one  quarter of an airline's operating budget  in the past airfare and oil prices move  together and savvy travelers could wait  for oil prices to drop before buying  airline tickets but two new studies both  show that's no longer the case if you  see oil prices jump higher there's no  need to wait to buy tickets and finally  number four buy on the weekend for years  Tuesday used to be the day to find the  cheapest tickets airline pricing  executives would come to work on Monday  tally up weekend sales and watch a sale  with newspaper ads on  Tuesday nowadays pricing is largely an  automated 24/7 business and airlines can  tailor their best prices to specific  days Expedia and AARC looked at about  half of all tickets sold in the US and  found that in general fares can be as  much as 20% lower if you buy on the  weekend and actually up to 36% lower if  you buy on a Sunday the highest prices  tend to be on Thursdays and Fridays  airlines know that business travellers  usually only buy during the work week  while the most price sensitive leisure  travelers buy on weekends other recent  studies by booking app hopper and a big  data analytics firm called three victors  looked at prices found in searches not  actual purchases they both found that  Tuesday's are sometimes still a good bet  since airlines often run flash sales  early in the week airline tickets are  volatile so it's still worthwhile to  search for deals every day but if you  see a good price on a Sunday grab it  it's likely to be the best price you're  going to see and if you're really  determined to find the lowest fare try  booking on the weekend about three or  four weeks before departure that's the  sweet spot so let's recap if you want  the lowest fare booked a nonstop flight  on a weekend at least three weeks before  departure regardless of oil prices and  make sure to stay over Saturday  [Music]
 

